(defmodule input
  "The editor's input layer: fold one key event into the next model.

Dispatch goes through **layers** (`std/layers.blsp`) composed across two scopes
(`ed-key-binding` → `scope-binding`): the active buffer's *mode* layers (`text-mode`
by default, see `modes`) over the model's keybinding *profile* layers (emacs/vim, see
`keymaps`), the buffer winning. `keymap-run` then runs one (possibly chorded)
keystroke through it, threading the prefix-`:pending` state — so the chord state
machine (prefix entry, mid-chord resolution, dead-end reset, self-insert fallback)
lives in the language toolkit, not here (the prime directive — see CLAUDE.md). The
bindings are data, declared per-mode in `modes`; this module only resolves the
active keymap, renders the echo-area label for a prefix / undefined chord, and
gates the minibuffer. Keys map to fully-qualified command **symbols** resolved
with `(reflect/eval sym)` at dispatch time, so redefining a command (or rebinding a key)
hot-swaps behaviour on the next keystroke (late binding, the path to a
self-editing editor)."
  (:use editor/buffer)
  (:use keyhelp) ; key / command labels, where-is, docstrings
  (:use scroll) ; ed-scroll / ed-max-top / ed-wrap? — keeping point on screen
  (:use model) ; message, the minibuffer field, current-buffer accessors, the registries
  (:use panes) ; ed-selected-text-rows (split-aware viewport) + ed-mouse (event folding)
  (:use hittest) ; ed-modeline-click / ed-modeline-hover / ed-diag-hover / ed-link-hover / ed-menu-hit — pointer hit-tests
  (:use commands) ; the self-insert fallback
  (:use menu) ; the right-click context menu: open / move / run / close (the `:menu` overlay)
  (:use complete-at-point) ; the completion popup (Tab) + its modal key handling
  (:use isearch) ; the modal incremental-search / query-replace mini-loops
  (:use interactive) ; current-prefix-arg / prefix-consuming? — the prefix-arg seam
  (:use editor/keymap) ; keymap-run — the std chord dispatcher's running half
  (:use editor/ui) ; overlay-route — the shared modal-overlay dispatch fallthrough
  ; bshell is loaded below with a bare `require`: its event handlers register at load,
  ; and its per-key prompt guard rides the generic `:post-key` layer facet now — nothing
  ; here refers to its names anymore
  ; compile is loaded below with a bare `require`: its defcommands + UiEvent impls
  ; register at load, but nothing here refers to its names anymore
  (:use hosted) ; hosted-step — the loop-tail content propagate for process-backed slots
  (:use collab) ; shared-buffer-process pushes: the [:buffer-updated …] event handler
  ; web / git / diffhl are deferred — each registers its idle-beat job when it loads
  (:use keymaps) ; ed-ensure-profile — seed the model-scope keybinding profile (emacs/vim)
  (:use editor/layers) ; scope-resolve / scope-binding (buffer + model layers) + init-buffer-layers
  (:use wrap)) ; rows-from — the last line a wrapped pane shows (ed-point-into-view)
;; `modes` defines the layers and registers the default (text-mode); the bindings live
;; there, as data, not here. It — with bshell (C-x p e shell), compile (M-x compile +
;; next-error) and git (idle-beat git-cache-status) — registers commands / event handlers
;; / UiEvent impls at load; as project source modules they load with the image.
;; editor/lineedit (the minibuffer's one-line editor core) is referenced qualified below
;; and auto-loads.
;; ---- the active keymap comes from the buffer's layers -----------------------

(defn- ed-ensure-layers (m)
  "Make sure both layer scopes are seeded before dispatch: the model's keybinding
*profile* (`keymaps/ed-ensure-profile` — emacs by default), and the current buffer's
*mode* layers (from its type — `text-mode` by default, `.blsp` → brood). Buffers
created anywhere get their mode here, lazily, so dispatch always has a keymap."
  (let (m1 (ed-ensure-profile m)
        b (ed-current-buf m1))
    (if (nil? (:layers b)) (ed-set-buf m1 (init-buffer-layers b)) m1)))

(defn ed-scope (m)
  "The scope contexts dispatch resolves through, highest precedence first: the selected
buffer's **mode** layers, then the model's **profile** layers. The profile (emacs/vim —
`keymaps`) lives on the model scope; the mode (brood/text/…) on the buffer.

Public and named because `help` reports on exactly this list (`C-h b` / `C-h k`), and a
second spelling of it there is the one way help could come to describe a keymap the
dispatcher is not using."
  (list (ed-current-buf m) m))

(defn- ed-key-binding (m key)
  "What `key` is bound to in the keymap in force — `ed-scope`'s layers, the buffer winning
(`scope-binding`). Asked per key rather than merging every layer's keymap into one map
first, which dispatch used to do on every keystroke to read one entry."
  (scope-binding (ed-scope m) key))

(defn- ed-post-key-guards (m-before m key)
  "Run the current buffer's mode `:post-key` guards over the just-dispatched model
(layers §7): each guard is `(m-before m-after key) -> model`, collected highest
precedence first, each seeing the previous guard's result. The model is immutable,
so a guard vetoes what the key did by returning `m-before` — how bshell keeps
point out of its prompt and the tutorial protects its prose without this dispatch
path naming either. Guards are fn symbols, resolved late (hot-swappable).

Then the `:on-move` FOLLOWERS (`ed-on-move-hooks`), which see only the guards' result:
a guard decides what the event did; a follower reacts to where the cursor ended up."
  (ed-on-move-hooks m-before
    (fold (layer-collect (ed-current-buf m) :post-key)
      m
      (fn (mm g) ((if (symbol? g) (reflect/eval g) g) m-before mm key)))))

(defn- ed-moved? (m-before m)
  "Did the event move the reader — a different buffer in the selected pane, or the
same buffer with its point or text changed (`buffer-moved?`)? The one test that gates
the `:on-move` facet, so no follower has to remember where it last was."
  (or (not (= (:buffer (ed-current-pane m-before)) (:buffer (ed-current-pane m))))
    (buffer-moved? (ed-current-buf m-before) (ed-current-buf m))))

(defn- ed-on-move-hooks (m-before m)
  "Run the current buffer's mode `:on-move` followers — each `(m) -> model`, collected
highest precedence first, each seeing the previous one's result — ONLY when the event
moved point or changed the text (`ed-moved?`). A follower keeps a companion pane on the
cursor (the markdown preview, the *Workings* pane); on the guard facet it re-asserted that
position on every event, including the wheel scrolling the very pane it follows with, and
snapped the reader's scroll back under them. Gating here, once, is what makes that class
of bug impossible to write: a follower never sees an event that moved nothing. Fn
symbols, resolved late (hot-swappable), like `:post-key`."
  (if (ed-moved? m-before m)
    (fold (layer-collect (ed-current-buf m) :on-move)
      m
      (fn (mm h) ((if (symbol? h) (reflect/eval h) h) mm)))
    m))

(defn ed-on-idle-hooks (m)
  "Run the current buffer's mode `:on-idle` hooks — each `(m) -> model`, collected highest
precedence first, each seeing the previous one's result — on the `:idle-recompute` beat,
once typing has settled. The per-mode half of that beat: the editor's own debounced work
(eldoc, diagnostics, the diff gutter) is the registered idle jobs; a mode's (the markdown
preview's re-render) rides this facet, so `:idle-recompute` names no mode. Fn symbols,
resolved late (hot-swappable), like `:post-key`."
  (fold (layer-collect (ed-current-buf m) :on-idle)
    m
    (fn (mm h) ((if (symbol? h) (reflect/eval h) h) mm))))

;; the mode's hooks run after the auto-save/revert and before the web mirror publishes, so
;; the snapshot shows what a hook re-rendered
(register-idle-job :mode-on-idle {:order 130 :run 'input/ed-on-idle-hooks})

;; ---- dispatch (the chord state machine is keymap-step; echo policy is ours) --
;; The echo-area key label is `keyhelp/ed-key-label` (shared with which-key + describe-key),
;; so a prefix / undefined-chord label reads identically everywhere (C-M-, S-, SPC, …).

(defn- ed-self-insert (m key)
  "The keymap fallback: a printable key self-inserts; any other unbound key is a
no-op (so a stray :f5 / :page-up never tries to insert a keyword).

In a modal COMMAND state — vim `:normal` or `:visual` — NOTHING types. An unbound
letter there is a dead key, exactly as in vim, where `z` alone is simply an incomplete
command. Without this the fallback typed every key vim does not bind: z, q, s, t, r,
m, f and most of the rest of the keyboard inserted themselves into the buffer, which
is what \"vim mode is very broken\" turns out to mean. `ed-vim-command-mode?` already
existed for the same reason — a mode layer's RET must not type in normal mode — but
the fallback, which is where the majority of keys land, never asked it."
  (cond
    (ed-vim-command-mode? m) m
    (string? key) (cmd-self-insert m key)
    else m))

;; ---- prefix arguments (C-u N) -----------------------------------------------
;; `:prefix-arg` is `{:count N :raw bool}` while a numeric argument is being built:
;; C-u seeds 4 (`:raw` — not yet explicit digits); a further C-u multiplies by 4; a
;; digit switches to explicit accumulation. The next *complete* command runs `:count`
;; times (the common-case Emacs mechanism; per-command interactive specs are the
;; deferred std/layers improvement). A command that reads the arg itself (C-u C-SPC →
;; pop-mark) registers via `interactive/register-prefix-consuming` (declared next to
;; the command) so it runs once, not repeated.

(defn- ed-digit? (input)
  "True if `input` is a single decimal-digit key."
  (and (string? input) (= (string/length input) 1) (>= (index-of "0123456789" input) 0)))

(defn- ed-prefix-continues? (m input)
  "Keys that extend a pending prefix arg rather than ending it: another C-u, a digit, or
a `-` **only while the argument is still raw** (no digits yet) — `C-u -` negates, but a
`-` after digits terminates the arg and self-inserts (so `C-u 5 -` types five dashes)."
  (or (= input :ctrl-u)
    (ed-digit? input)
    (and (= input "-") (get-in m [:prefix-arg :raw]))))

(defn- ed-prefix-read (m input)
  "Fold a C-u / digit / `-` into the active prefix-arg accumulator. The count carries its
sign, so digits after a negative seed (from `M--`/`C--` or `-`) accumulate negative."
  (let (pa (:prefix-arg m)
        c (:count pa)
        neg (< c 0))
    (cond
      (= input :ctrl-u) (assoc m :prefix-arg {:count (* 4 c) :raw true})
      ;; `-`: raw → seed a negative argument (-1); mid-number → flip the sign.
      (= input "-") (assoc m :prefix-arg {:count (if (:raw pa) -1 (- c)) :raw (:raw pa)})
      else
      ;; A digit, by `ed-prefix-continues?` at the only call site — but that invariant
      ;; lives in another function, and `string/->number` answers `number | failure`.
      ;; An unparseable key leaves the accumulator alone rather than raising in `-`/`+`.
        (let (d (string/->number input))
          (if (failure? d)
            m
            (let (sd (if neg (- d) d))
              (assoc m
                :prefix-arg
                  (if (:raw pa) {:count sd :raw false} {:count (+ (* 10 c) sd) :raw false}))))))))

(sig ed-run-arg (model any any any int -> model))
;; check-allow: the inverse command is resolved late (`reflect/eval`); its result is the model
;; every command answers
(check-allow :trusted
  (defn- ed-run-arg (m1 step this-cmd input n)
    "Run `this-cmd` under numeric prefix argument `n`: `|n|` repetitions, swapping to its
registered inverse command when `n` is negative (Emacs negative-argument — `C-- C-f`
goes backward). Repeats thread `:last-command`/`:this-command` so consecutive kills /
inserts coalesce. With no registered inverse a negative arg just repeats `|n|` times."
    (let (an (math/abs n)
          inv (command-inverse this-cmd))
      (if (and (< n 0) inv)
        ;; the inverse runs directly, not through the keymap, so it does what a keymap step
        ;; does around a command itself: the echo starts clean, and the chord that led here
        ;; is over — a `C-u - M-g n` left `:pending` on the M-g map, and the next `n`
        ;; ran next-error instead of typing
        (assoc (fold (range an)
                 m1
                 (fn (mm i)
                   ((reflect/eval inv) (assoc mm
                                         :last-command (if (= i 0) (:last-command mm) inv)
                                         :this-command inv
                                         :echo "")
                     input)))
          :pending nil)
        (fold (range an) m1 (fn (mm i) (step (if (= i 0) mm (assoc mm :last-command this-cmd)))))))))

(defn- ed-amalgamate-undo? (this last)
  "Whether `this` command's edit amalgamates into the previous undo group: it repeats
`last` and both are typing/deletion run commands (`model/*typing-commands*`). Drives
`amalgamate-undo`."
  (and (= this last) (includes? *typing-commands* this)))

(def- ed-shift-deactivators
  ;; plain motions that collapse a shift-selection (Emacs shift-select-mode): a non-shift
  ;; arrow/word/buffer move clears the region and just moves. Region-using commands (kill,
  ;; copy, …) aren't here, so they still act on a shift-selected region.
  (list 'commands/cmd-forward-char
    'commands/cmd-backward-char 'commands/cmd-next-line 'commands/cmd-previous-line
    'commands/cmd-beginning-of-line 'commands/cmd-end-of-line 'commands/cmd-forward-word
    'commands/cmd-backward-word 'commands/cmd-beginning-of-buffer 'commands/cmd-end-of-buffer))

(defn- ed-shift-deactivate (m this-cmd)
  "Collapse a shift-selection when a plain motion runs: clear the mark + `:shift-select`
flag so a non-shift arrow moves without a region (Emacs shift-select-mode). Other
commands leave the region intact (so kill/copy act on a shift-selection)."
  (if (and (:shift-select m) (includes? ed-shift-deactivators this-cmd))
    (ed-set-buf (dissoc m :shift-select) (clear-mark (ed-current-buf m)))
    m))

(defn ed-press-prefix (m key)
  "The model as if prefix `key` (`:ctrl-c`) had just been pressed: the NEXT key resolves
in that prefix's map — Emacs `set-transient-map`. For a command standing in for a prefix
the buffer cannot type: a terminal buffer's `C-x c` is its `C-c` map, because the program
owns C-c itself. A key that is no prefix anywhere is a no-op.

The map is the MODEL scope's (the profile's: `C-c t` runs the tests) with the buffer's own
additions merged over it. Not the composed keymap the dispatcher uses, because layers
merge shallowly and a chord belongs to its winning layer (std/editor/layers) — the term
mode binds `C-c C-j`, which takes the whole `C-c` map with it, and a command whose entire
purpose is to reach the editor's map would hand back only the two keys the mode added."
  (let (profile (scope-binding (list m) key)
        mode (ed-key-binding m key)
        sub (merge (if (map? profile) profile {}) (if (map? mode) mode {})))
    (if (empty? sub) m (assoc m :prefix-map sub :prefix-key key))))

(defn- ed-binding-command (binding)
  "The command `keymap-step` will run for `binding`: the symbol when bound, `:self-insert`
when unbound (the fallback), nil for a prefix key (mid-chord — no command)."
  (cond
    (map? binding) nil
    (nil? binding) :self-insert
    :else binding))

(defn- ed-begin-command (m this-cmd)
  "The model as a command `this-cmd` is about to run: `:last-command`/`:this-command`
recorded (the Emacs mechanism) so kills coalesce and M-y yank-pop knows it follows a yank;
the current buffer's undo amalgamation armed — a typing / deletion run merges into one undo
group, every other command starts a fresh one (so a command undoes atomically — Emacs
`undo-boundary`), set explicitly each command so a no-op edit can't leave a stale flag; and
a shift-selection collapsed when the command is a plain motion."
  (let (amalgamate (and (ed-amalgamate-undo? this-cmd (:this-command m)) (not (:undo-break m)))
        started (assoc (dissoc m :undo-break)
                  :last-command (:this-command m)
                  :this-command this-cmd))
    (ed-shift-deactivate (ed-set-buf started
                           (assoc (ed-current-buf started) :undo-amalgamate amalgamate))
      this-cmd)))

(defn- ed-keymap-step (resolved input m)
  "One keymap step: run `input`'s `resolved` binding (`scope-resolve` against `:pending`)
through `keymap-run`, threading `:pending`. A command that PRESSED a prefix
(`ed-press-prefix`) leaves the next key to resolve in that sub-map, and the chord reads as
the prefix."
  (let ([stepped pending] (keymap-run resolved (:pending m) (assoc m :echo "") input ed-self-insert)
        pressed (:prefix-map stepped))
    (if (nil? pressed)
      (assoc stepped :pending pending)
      (assoc (dissoc stepped :prefix-map :prefix-key)
        :pending pressed
        :chord [(:prefix-key stepped)]
        :echo (str (ed-keys-label [(:prefix-key stepped)]) "-")))))

(defn- ed-run-command (m this-cmd input step)
  "Run the started command `this-cmd` through `step`, under the numeric prefix argument
when one is set, and clear the argument once it has been consumed."
  (let (repeat-count (current-prefix-arg m))
    (cond
      (nil? repeat-count) (step m)
      ;; a command that consumes the arg itself (set-mark → pop-mark): run once
      (prefix-consuming? this-cmd) (dissoc (step m) :prefix-arg)
      ;; a normal command under a prefix arg: run |N| times — the inverse when N is
      ;; negative (`ed-run-arg`). Every repetition resolves `input` in the SAME map the
      ;; first did: the first step ends the chord, so a bare `step` would look the last key
      ;; up at top level — `C-u 3 C-x o` switched pane once and then typed `oo`
      else
        (dissoc (ed-run-arg m
                  (fn (mm) (step (assoc mm :pending (:pending m))))
                  this-cmd
                  input
                  repeat-count)
          :prefix-arg))))

(defn- ed-echo-chord (m binding result input)
  "`result` with the echo area showing the chord so far. `:chord` accumulates the keys of the
in-progress prefix chord, so the echo shows the FULL chord (\"C-x p-\", \"C-x p e is
undefined\") — not just the last key. Carried while a prefix is pending; cleared the moment
the chord resolves (a command ran) or aborts."
  (let (chord (append (or (:chord m) []) [input]))
    (cond
      (map? binding) (assoc result :echo (str (ed-keys-label chord) "-") :chord chord)
      (and (nil? binding) (:pending m))
        (assoc result :echo (str (ed-keys-label chord) " is undefined") :chord [])
      ;; the command pressed a prefix: the step set the chord and echo to it
      (:pending result) result
      else (assoc result :chord []))))

(defn- ed-dispatch-key (m input)
  "Resolve and run `input` through the active keymap (the chord work is `keymap-step`),
repeating the command `:prefix-arg` times when one is set (negative runs the inverse —
see `ed-run-arg`). A prefix key mid-chord just steps (the prefix arg carries until the
chord completes); a prefix-consuming command (set-mark) runs once and reads the arg."
  (let (pending (:pending m)
        binding (if (nil? pending) (ed-key-binding m input) (get pending input))
        ;; resolved ONCE: every repetition under a prefix arg steps from this same
        ;; `:pending` (`ed-run-command`), so it would find this same binding
        resolved (keymap-classify binding)
        this-cmd (ed-binding-command binding)
        step (fn (mm) (ed-keymap-step resolved input mm))
        result (cond
                 ;; a prefix key mid-chord: step, keep the prefix arg for the resolving key
                 (nil? this-cmd) (step m)
                 ;; a guest's session runs only what edits the buffers it was given
                 (and (:guest-session m) (not (ed-guest-allows? this-cmd)))
                   (ed-guest-refusal (dissoc m :pending :prefix-arg))
                 else (ed-run-command (ed-begin-command m this-cmd) this-cmd input step)))
    (ed-echo-chord m binding result input)))

;; ---- repeat-mode (Emacs `repeat-mode`: tap the last key to repeat the command) -----
;; After a command in `*repeat-commands*` runs (typically via a chord like C-x o), arm a
;; `:repeat` transient: pressing the same key again re-invokes the command, and again, …
;; until any other key (which exits and dispatches normally). This is how a chord command
;; repeats — Emacs never hold-repeats chords; it taps. The `:repeat` overlay lives in
;; `ed-transient-handlers`; arming happens here, post-dispatch.

(def *repeat-commands*
  ;; Commands that arm repeat-mode. Add a command symbol here to make it tap-repeatable:
  ;; C-x o then `o o o` cycles panes; C-x → then `→ → →` cycles buffers.
  (list 'commands/cmd-other-pane
    'commands/cmd-next-buffer 'commands/cmd-previous-buffer 'commands/cmd-repeat))

(defn- ed-arm-repeat-mode (m input)
  "After `ed-dispatch-key`, arm `:repeat` (repeat-mode) when this step actually ran a
command (`:pending` is clear — not a mid-chord prefix) that's in `*repeat-commands*`, so
pressing `input` (its last key) again re-invokes it. Otherwise leave the model as-is (the
`:repeat` overlay's `:exit` has already cleared any prior repeat on a non-repeat key)."
  (if (and (nil? (:pending m)) (includes? *repeat-commands* (:this-command m)))
    (assoc m :repeat {:key input :command (:this-command m)})
    m))

(defn- ed-repeat-run (m)
  "Re-invoke the armed repeat-mode command (an Emacs repeat-mode tap), keeping `:repeat`
armed so it keeps repeating. Resolves the command symbol to its fn and runs it with the
repeat key; the command itself does the work (pane/buffer cycling, …)."
  (let (rp (:repeat m))
    ((reflect/eval (:command rp)) m (:key rp))))

(defn ed-dispatch (m0 input)
  "Fold one key `input` into the next model, recording it in the lossage ring first (`C-h l`
sees every key that reached here, bound or not). While a numeric prefix argument is being
built (`:prefix-arg` set and `input` a C-u/digit), accumulate it and run nothing yet;
otherwise resolve and run the key through the active keymap (`ed-dispatch-key`)."
  (let (m (help/lossage-record (ed-ensure-layers m0) input))
    (if (and (:prefix-arg m) (ed-prefix-continues? m input))
      (let (m2 (ed-prefix-read m input))
        (assoc m2 :echo (str "C-u " (get-in m2 [:prefix-arg :count]) "-")))
      (ed-arm-repeat-mode (ed-dispatch-key m input) input))))

;; ---- the minibuffer (modal: when active, all input is the minibuffer's) ------
;; A one-line prompt that REUSES the REPL line editor: `:minibuffer :edit` is a
;; `std/lineedit` state (the same pure core std/observer's minibuffer drives), so
;; the prompt gets emacs editing for free — C-a/C-e, C-f/C-b, M-f/M-b, C-k/C-u/C-w/
;; M-d, C-y yank, C-t, ↑/↓ history, C-r reverse-search. Keys are fed to
;; `lineedit-handle`; we intercept only **Tab** (per-prompt completion via the
;; minibuffer's `:complete-fn`, not lineedit's symbol completion) and **Esc / C-g**
;; (abort). Enter makes lineedit set `:done :submit`, and we run `:on-complete` with
;; the text; C-c (`:cancel`) closes. (The text/point/prompt accessors live in `model`.)

(defn- mb-preview (m cand)
  "Fire the active minibuffer's optional `:on-preview` hook with the currently-selected
candidate `cand`, returning the updated model. The hook previews the selection (e.g. imenu
jumps point to that symbol) *without* closing the prompt; absent hook or nil candidate is a
no-op. The post-step `ed-scroll` then brings the previewed point into view."
  (let (hook (get-in m [:minibuffer :on-preview]))
    (if (or (nil? hook) (nil? cand)) m (hook m cand))))

(defn- mb-close (m)
  "The model with the minibuffer closed — the ONE place a prompt goes away, so the things a
prompt owns are released exactly once. Today that is its ranking session
(`model/ed-mb-stop-ranker`): a process that outlives its prompt is a leak, and a prompt is
closed from four places (abort, Enter on a selection, Enter on the typed text, lineedit's
own `:submit`), which is three too many to remember it in."
  (assoc (ed-mb-stop-ranker m) :minibuffer nil))

(defn- mb-abort (m)
  "Close the minibuffer, first firing its optional `:on-abort` hook so a previewing prompt
can restore the position it started from (the imenu cancel-returns-home behaviour)."
  (let (hook (get-in m [:minibuffer :on-abort])
        m2 (if (nil? hook) m (hook m)))
    (assoc (mb-close m2) :echo "quit")))

(defn- mb-with-edit (m edit)
  "The model with the minibuffer's line replaced by lineedit state `edit`. The selection
goes back to where a fresh list starts (`mb-sel`: the first row, or the prompt itself): the
line changed, so the candidate list did too."
  (assoc m :minibuffer (dissoc (assoc (:minibuffer m) :edit edit) :sel)))

(defn- mb-set-line (m text)
  "The model with the minibuffer's line replaced by `text`, point at its end."
  (mb-with-edit m (ed-mb-set-text (get-in m [:minibuffer :edit]) text)))

(defn- mb-choices (mb)
  "The candidates a selection can be made among: the candidate list of a prompt that
accepts a selection (Vertico-style — M-x, C-x b, C-x p f), else `()`."
  (if (:accept-selection mb) (mb-candidates mb) ()))

(defn- mb-chosen (mb choices)
  "The candidate the selection rests on, clamped to the list (it can be a row past the end
after the list shrank) — or nil when there is nothing to choose among, or the selection is
on the prompt itself (`mb-sel` -1), so the typed text is what is meant."
  (let (sel (mb-sel mb))
    (if (or (empty? choices) (< sel 0)) nil (nth choices (math/min sel (dec (count choices)))))))

(defn- mb-moved-sel (mb size motion)
  "The row a list-motion key moves the selection to. From the prompt (-1), `C-p` goes to the
last row as Vertico's does; everything else is `editor/list-motion`'s."
  (let (sel (mb-sel mb))
    (if (and (< sel 0) (= motion :previous))
      (dec size)
      (editor/list-motion/motion-index sel size motion))))

(defn- mb-select (m choices index)
  "Move the selection to row `index` of `choices`, firing `:on-preview` with the candidate
there so a previewing prompt (imenu) tracks the highlight."
  (mb-preview (assoc-in m [:minibuffer :sel] index) (nth choices index)))

(defn- mb-yank (m)
  "C-y pastes the EDITOR's kill ring (and the OS clipboard) into the line — the minibuffer
is where a search string or a path gets pasted; the line editor's own C-y knows only what
C-k/C-w cut from the line, which is never what a paste means."
  (let ([m1 text] (ed-kill-head m))
    (if (nil? text)
      (message m1 "kill ring is empty")
      (mb-with-edit m1 (editor/lineedit/lineedit-insert (get-in m1 [:minibuffer :edit]) text)))))

(defn- mb-complete (m choices)
  "Tab on a Vertico prompt with candidates fills the **selected** candidate (the highlighted
row), not the common prefix of the typed text — so Tab on a hovered directory completes THAT
directory (its trailing \"/\" then re-lists its contents = expands it) instead of snapping
the selection back to the top. With no candidates (a freshly typed path), fall back to the
prompt's own `:complete-fn`; a prompt with nothing to complete against (compile, a preset
name) leaves the text as typed — Tab used to call a nil `:complete-fn` and echo an error."
  (let (mb (:minibuffer m)
        chosen (mb-chosen mb choices))
    (mb-set-line m
      (if (nil? chosen) ((or (:complete-fn mb) identity) (mb-text mb)) chosen))))

(defn- mb-submit (m text)
  "Close the prompt and run its `:on-complete` with `text`."
  ((get-in m [:minibuffer :on-complete]) (mb-close m) text))

(defn- mb-line-edit (m input)
  "Every other key drives lineedit's pure editor: on `:submit` (Enter) run `:on-complete`
with the text, on `:cancel` close, else keep editing (typing resets the selection)."
  (let (edit (editor/lineedit/lineedit-handle (get-in m [:minibuffer :edit]) input)
        done (:done edit))
    (cond
      (nil? done) (mb-with-edit m edit)
      (= done :submit) (mb-submit m (:text edit))
      else (mb-abort m))))

(defn minibuffer-handle (m input)
  "Fold one key into the active minibuffer. C-y pastes the kill ring; Tab completes
(`mb-complete`); Esc / C-g abort (firing `:on-abort`); a list-motion key (`editor/list-motion`
— C-n/↓, C-p/↑, M-< / M->) moves the selection and fires `:on-preview`; Enter accepts the
selected candidate, else the typed text; every other key drives lineedit's pure editor
(`mb-line-edit`)."
  ;; No forced re-rank on Tab/Enter/↑/↓ any more. It existed because a large source was
  ;; DEBOUNCED onto the idle beat and could be showing a stale list when you acted on it —
  ;; so a selection move ranked the whole set synchronously, which on a 27k-file project
  ;; cost 210 ms per ↓ and made holding it useless. A session ranks off the loop, so the
  ;; list is never more than one in-flight answer behind and a selection move is free.
  (let (mb (:minibuffer m)
        choices (mb-choices mb)
        motion (editor/list-motion/motion-of input))
    (cond
      (= input :ctrl-y) (mb-yank m)
      (= input :tab) (mb-complete m choices)
      (ed-cancel-key? input) (mb-abort m)
      ;; a selection move needs a list to move in: with none, C-n/C-p/↑/↓ fall through to
      ;; lineedit's history. M-< / M-> jump to the ends of the LIST, the way they jump to
      ;; the ends of a buffer — falling through to lineedit read them as ordinary text, so a
      ;; long imenu could only be crossed a row at a time.
      (and motion (not (empty? choices)))
        (mb-select m choices (mb-moved-sel mb (count choices) motion))
      ;; Enter accepts the *selected candidate* (Vertico prompt with candidates), else the
      ;; typed text (find-file, or no candidates).
      (= input :enter)
        (let (chosen (mb-chosen mb choices))
          (mb-submit m (if (nil? chosen) (mb-text mb) chosen)))
      else (mb-line-edit m input))))

;; `ed-cancel-key?` moved to `model` — it is key vocabulary, like `ed-key-label`, and
;; `transient` needs it too. It cannot live here: `input` owns the overlay that drives a
;; transient menu, so a transient module reaching back into `input` for one predicate
;; would close a cycle around the whole dispatcher.

;; ---- transient input overlays (one registry; std's fallthrough policy) ------
;; The minibuffer, the completion popup, incremental search, and query-replace are
;; all *transient* modes that sit beside the keymap: while open, one of them captures
;; input. Each is a record implementing `editor/ui`'s `Overlay` ability (ADR-183):
;; `overlay-active?` says when it is open, `overlay-owns?` which keys are its to
;; handle (skipped = capture-all, like the minibuffer), `overlay-handle` folds an
;; owned key into the model, and `overlay-exit` dismisses before a non-owned key
;; falls through (skipped = no dismiss step). The shared `editor/ui/overlay-route`
;; routes one key to whichever is open. A new modal feature defines a record + impl
;; and joins `ed-transient-handlers` instead of growing `ed-update`. The dispatch
;; rule itself lives in std (ADR-094) — the observer's minibuffer rides the same
;; `overlay-route` — and re-registering an impl hot-swaps a live overlay (late
;; binding, same as commands).

(defn ed-describe-key-step (m input)
  "One key of a C-h k key sequence: resolve `input` through the active keymap (descending
into a pending prefix). On a command, report its name + first docstring line; on a prefix,
keep reading; on an unbound key, say so. Esc/C-g aborts."
  (if (ed-cancel-key? input)
    (message (dissoc m :describe-key) "quit")
    (let (st (:describe-key m)
          keys (append (:keys st) (list input))
          binding (if (nil? (:pending st)) (ed-key-binding m input) (get (:pending st) input)))
      (cond
        (map? binding) (assoc m :describe-key {:pending binding :keys keys})
        (nil? binding) (message (dissoc m :describe-key) (str (ed-keys-label keys) " is undefined"))
        :else
          (message (dissoc m :describe-key)
            (str (ed-keys-label keys)
              " runs "
              (ed-cmd-label binding)
              ;; which LAYER bound it — the half of `C-h k` that answers "why this and not
              ;; the one I expected". `help/binding-layer` asks `scope-bindings`, the same
              ;; walk dispatch resolved through a moment ago, so the two cannot disagree.
              (let (layer (help/binding-layer m (vec keys)))
                (if (nil? layer) "" (str " (" (->string layer) " layer)")))
              (let (d (ed-doc binding)) (if d (str " — " (ed-first-line d)) ""))))))))

(defrecord describe-key-overlay ()) ; C-h k: the next key sequence is described, not executed
(impl Overlay
  describe-key-overlay
  (overlay-active? [_ m] (:describe-key m))
  ;; no overlay-owns? — capture-all: the whole next chord is ours to resolve
  (overlay-handle [_ m input] (ed-describe-key-step m input)))

(defrecord read-char-overlay ()) ; ed-read-char: the next key is handed to a continuation (zap, registers)
(impl Overlay
  read-char-overlay
  (overlay-active? [_ m] (:read-char m))
  ;; no overlay-owns? — capture-all: the next key is the one being read
  (overlay-handle [_ m input]
    (if (ed-cancel-key? input)
      (message (dissoc m :read-char) "quit")
      ((get-in m [:read-char :then]) (dissoc m :read-char) input))))

(defrecord transient-overlay ()) ; a transient menu (Magit's `P`): every key is the menu's until it closes
(impl Overlay
  transient-overlay
  ;; the flag is checked INLINE so a key in an editor that has never opened a menu never
  ;; loads transient.blsp — the deferred-module rule `ed-terminals?` states for terminals
  (overlay-active? [_ m] (:transient m))
  ;; no overlay-owns? — modal: a menu binds its own keys and answers `:unbound` for the
  ;; rest, which is how it decides for itself what an unknown key means
  (overlay-handle [_ m input] (transient/ed-transient-key m input)))

(defrecord menu-overlay ()) ; the right-click context menu: arrows move, M-<|M-> jump, Enter runs, Esc closes
(impl Overlay
  menu-overlay
  (overlay-active? [_ m] (:menu m))
  ;; no overlay-owns? — modal: every key is the menu's while it's open
  (overlay-handle [_ m input]
    (let (motion (editor/list-motion/motion-of input))
      (cond
        motion (ed-menu-motion m motion)
        (= input :enter) (ed-menu-run m)
        (ed-cancel-key? input) (ed-close-menu m)
        ;; any other key is swallowed (the menu stays open)
        else m))))
(defrecord query-replace-overlay ())
(impl Overlay
  query-replace-overlay
  (overlay-active? [_ m] (:query-replace m))
  (overlay-owns? [_ m input] (qr-control-key? input))
  (overlay-handle [_ m input] (qr-handle m input))
  (overlay-exit [_ m] (qr-finish m)))

(defrecord isearch-overlay ())
(impl Overlay
  isearch-overlay
  (overlay-active? [_ m] (:isearch m))
  (overlay-owns? [_ m input] (isearch-control-key? input))
  (overlay-handle [_ m input] (isearch-handle m input))
  (overlay-exit [_ m] (isearch-exit m)))

(defrecord minibuffer-overlay ())
(impl Overlay
  minibuffer-overlay
  (overlay-active? [_ m] (:minibuffer m))
  ;; no overlay-owns? — capture-all: every key is the minibuffer's
  (overlay-handle [_ m input] (minibuffer-handle m input)))

(defrecord completion-overlay ())
(impl Overlay
  completion-overlay
  (overlay-active? [_ m] (:completion m))
  (overlay-owns? [_ m input] (complete-control-key? input))
  (overlay-handle [_ m input] (complete-handle m input))
  (overlay-exit [_ m] (complete-cancel m)))

(defrecord preview-overlay ()) ; the inline completion preview: M-n / M-p cycle the candidate
(impl Overlay
  preview-overlay
  (overlay-active? [_ m] (:completion-preview m))
  (overlay-owns? [_ m input] (complete-at-point/preview-control-key? input))
  (overlay-handle [_ m input] (complete-at-point/preview-handle m input)))
;; No `overlay-exit`, deliberately. Every other overlay here is a MODE you leave — the
;; popup closes, isearch ends — and a key it does not own dismisses it on the way past. A
;; preview is not a mode: it is a suggestion sitting beside what you are typing, and the
;; next keystroke should keep typing and let the idle beat recompute it, not dismiss it.

(defn- ed-term-buffer? (m)
  "Whether the selected pane shows a terminal buffer in CHAR mode (`terminal.blsp`): the
program's keys. A term in line mode is edited like a shell, through the keymaps."
  (let (b (ed-current-buf m)) (and (= (:type b) :term) (not (:term-line-mode b)))))

(defrecord term-overlay ()) ; a terminal buffer: every key is the program's but C-x (the editor's prefix) and C-y (paste into it)
(impl Overlay
  term-overlay
  (overlay-active? [_ m] (and (nil? (:pending m)) (ed-term-buffer? m)))
  (overlay-owns? [_ m input] (not (includes? [:ctrl-x :ctrl-y] input)))
  (overlay-handle [_ m input] (terminal/term-send-key m input)))
;; No `overlay-exit`: C-x falling through starts a chord, and the buffer is still a terminal.

(defrecord repeat-overlay ()) ; Emacs repeat-mode: tap the last key again to repeat the command
(impl Overlay
  repeat-overlay
  (overlay-active? [_ m] (:repeat m))
  (overlay-owns? [_ m input] (= input (get-in m [:repeat :key])))
  (overlay-handle [_ m input] (ed-repeat-run m))
  (overlay-exit [_ m] (dissoc m :repeat)))

(def- ed-transient-handlers
  (list (describe-key-overlay)
    (read-char-overlay)
    (menu-overlay)
    (query-replace-overlay)
    (isearch-overlay)
    (minibuffer-overlay)
    (completion-overlay)
    ;; BELOW the minibuffer, and that placement is load-bearing: reading an option's value
    ;; (`:read`) leaves the menu on the model *and* opens a minibuffer, so if a transient
    ;; outranked it the menu would eat the keys meant for the prompt. Under it, the prompt
    ;; takes them and the menu resumes when it closes — with the value set.
    (transient-overlay)
    ;; a terminal owns its keys, but the minibuffer and the popups above it win when open
    (term-overlay)
    ;; below everything you deliberately opened (the popup, a menu), and below a terminal:
    ;; the preview has no exit, so a key it does not own skips every overlay under it
    ;; straight to dispatch. Above the terminal, it sent a char-mode program's keys to
    ;; self-insert in its read-only buffer for as long as a suggestion stood — and none
    ;; reached the program to change the screen and clear it
    (preview-overlay)
    (repeat-overlay)))

(defn ed-route-transient (m input)
  "Fold `input` into the active transient overlay, or through normal dispatch
(`ed-dispatch`) when none is open — via the shared `editor/ui/overlay-route`. The
owning handler consumes the key; otherwise the overlay exits (`:exit`) and the key is
dispatched normally (Emacs' dismiss-and-process)."
  (overlay-route ed-transient-handlers m input ed-dispatch))

;; ---- key repeat: the desktop's, not ours (brood ADR-441) ---------------------
;; The window is opened with `{:key-repeat :os}`: a held key's auto-repeats arrive as
;; plain presses at the delay and rate the user set for their desktop (GNOME's keyboard
;; settings, `xset r rate`, KDE's), with at most one ever queued and that one taken back at
;; the release, so the screen stops with the key even behind a slow frame. Each repeat is
;; dispatched like the press it repeats: a chord's last key repeats as itself (C-x o o o,
;; which repeat-mode answers), and a mode's `:post-key` guards see every one.
;;
;; bedit keeps no state about a held key: every repeat is a real press (and the window
;; restarts its caret blink on it). Every timer that waits for a pause is `:idle?`,
;; and `ui-run` holds those while a key is down, the delay before the first repeat
;; included, so the idle beat, a live-eval debounce and the typing undo boundary all
;; wait for the key to come up.

(defn ed-key-step (m input textrows textcols)
  "Route one key through normal dispatch, then re-clamp scroll (vertical + horizontal so a
long line follows point). A press and each of its OS auto-repeats take this same step, so
the mode `:post-key` guards see every one: holding backspace in a tutorial box stops at its
╭─ border instead of eating into the read-only prose. The guards run before the scroll
clamp, so a veto's restored point is what the viewport follows."
  (let (guarded (ed-clamp-restriction (ed-adjust-restriction m
                                        (ed-post-key-guards m (ed-route-transient m input) input))))
    (ed-scroll-horizontal (ed-scroll guarded textrows) textcols)))

;; ---- named timers (ADR-102): firing the model's declared `:timers` -------------
;; `ed-update` routes a `[:timer name]` firing here. The arming side (which timers are
;; present) lives in `model/ed-sync-timers`; this is the dispatch for what each does when
;; it fires. Note `[:timer …]` is a vector, so its arm must precede `ed-system-event?`
;; (else it'd fall through to key dispatch); `ed-macro-record` already skips vectors.

(defn- ed-timer-event? (input)
  "True if `input` is a named-timer firing — a `[:timer name]` vector (ADR-102)."
  (and (vector? input) (= (first input) :timer)))

(def- *cosmetic-timers*
  ;; The timers whose firing changes only how the frame LOOKS — the spinner frame, the
  ;; scrollbar's opacity — and nothing any other part of the turn reads: no text, no
  ;; point, no scroll, no pane. `ed-update` folds them through `ed-cosmetic-fire` alone
  ;; and skips the rest of the pipeline (timer sync, post steps, span refresh, the hosted
  ;; propagate, the collab chain), which on a 16 ms fade tick was a whole editor turn for
  ;; an opacity that only had to drop. Each one keeps its own timer honest: the fade drops
  ;; itself when it reaches zero, the hold by starting the fade; the spinner is dropped by
  ;; the turn that finishes the work (`model/ed-sync-timers`), which is never a cosmetic
  ;; one. The caret blink is not here at all: the window blinks it (brood ADR-469).
  #{:spinner :scrollbar-hold :scrollbar-fade})

(defn- ed-cosmetic-timer? (input)
  "True if `input` fires one of the [[*cosmetic-timers*]]."
  (and (ed-timer-event? input) (contains? *cosmetic-timers* (nth input 1))))

(defn- ed-cosmetic-fire (m name)
  "Service a fired [[*cosmetic-timers*]] entry: `:spinner` advances the activity frame,
`:scrollbar-hold` starts the thumb's fade and `:scrollbar-fade` ticks it down."
  (cond
    ;; the activity spinner: advance the frame, nothing else. The segment reads the
    ;; counter; when the work finishes `ed-sync-timers` drops this timer entirely.
    (= name :spinner)
      (let (n (:spin m 0)
            next (if (int? n) (inc n) 1))
        ; the field is `any` off the model, like every other read
        (assoc m :spin (math/mod next 1000)))
    ;; The overlay scrollbar: the quiet hold ends by starting the fade ticker, which ticks
    ;; the visibility budget down; dragging pins the thumb, and hitting zero hides it and
    ;; stops the timer so the editor idles again (no wakeups at rest).
    (= name :scrollbar-hold) (ed-scrollbar-start-fade m)
    (= name :scrollbar-fade)
      (if (:sb-drag m)
        (assoc m :sb-fade *scrollbar-fade-ticks*)
        (let (budget (dec (or (:sb-fade m) 0)))
          (if (<= budget 0)
            (ed-drop-timer (assoc m :sb-fade 0) :scrollbar-fade)
            (assoc m :sb-fade budget))))
    else m))

(defn- ed-timer-fire (m name textrows textcols cols rows)
  "Service the fired timer `name`: `:idle-recompute` runs the debounced eldoc cache + diagnostics check; `:which-key` reveals
the prefix panel; `:undo-pause` closes the typing run (next edit starts a fresh undo
group); a cosmetic one (the caret, the spinner, the scrollbar) is `ed-cosmetic-fire`'s. An
unknown name is a no-op."
  (cond
    ;; the idle debounce: eldoc, the diagnostics check, a large completion source's
    ;; re-rank, the off-the-loop diff-hl launch, the web-mirror push… — all the work too
    ;; costly for the per-key path, run once activity settles. Each is a row its module
    ;; registered (`model/register-idle-job`), run in the table's order.
    ;; In a live loop the beat runs in slices so a key never waits for all of it; headless
    ;; (tests, a batch run) it runs whole, as before.
    (= name :idle-recompute) (if (ed-headless?) (ed-run-idle-jobs m) (ed-run-idle-slice m 0))
    (= name :idle-continue) (ed-run-idle-slice m (or (:idle-resume m) 0))
    ;; a timer a feature registered (`model/register-timer-client`) — git's upstream watch,
    ;; a displayed BEAM view re-asking its node, a collaborator's name tag fading
    (contains? (ed-timer-clients) name)
      (ed-call-deferred m (get-in (ed-timer-clients) [name :fire]) m)
    (contains? *cosmetic-timers* name) (ed-cosmetic-fire m name)
    (= name :which-key) (ed-which-key-reveal m)
    ;; the glide stopped: ease every pane onto a whole line (`scroll/ed-glide-scroll-step`)
    (= name :scroll-settle) (assoc m :scroll-gliding true)
    (= name :scroll-glide) (ed-glide-scroll-step m)
    (= name :undo-pause) (ed-break-undo-run m)
    ;; A live-evaluating buffer's debounce landed: the tutorial's lesson boxes, the Brood
    ;; playground, the Elixir playground. Re-evaluate the forms this edit could have
    ;; changed the answer of. All three are rows in `model/ed-live-eval-clients` — each
    ;; deferred-loaded and late-bound, the keymap idiom — so a fourth language that
    ;; evaluates as you type is a table entry rather than another arm here.
    (includes? (map (ed-live-eval-clients) :timer) name) (ed-live-eval-launch m name)
    else m))

;; ---- inbound system / async events (the loop's event bus) -------------------
;; Besides keys, the ui-run loop receives async messages on its mailbox. An event the
;; editor PRODUCES ITSELF is a record implementing model's `UiEvent` ability — the
;; producer module defines the record + impl and just sends it; `ed-update` folds any
;; such record via `ui-event-fold`, so a new async producer (an LSP client, a
;; file-watcher, a buffer-process stream) registers an impl rather than editing the
;; loop or this file. What remains BELOW is the residual `[:tag …]` vector registry,
;; for events whose producer we don't own: redirected output (`:io/write` — std/io /
;; std/log), a spawned eval's reply (`:task-done` / `:task-error` / `:task-timeout`,
;; std/task), the frontend's `:resize` / `:mouse` / `:key-up`, the runtime's `:down`,
;; std's `:buffer-updated` / `:client-opts`, and the lsp reply tags (vectors because
;; completion's bounded wait selectively receives `[:lsp-completion ^corr …]`). Each
;; handler does its own scroll-clamp (output/eval don't move point; resize/mouse do).

(defn- ed-ev-io-write (m input textrows cols rows)
  "Redirected output (`[:io/write s]`) → *Messages* + the echo area (the default
output sink: eval stdout, the uncategorised logger backend)."
  (message m (string/trimr (nth input 1))))

(defn- ed-ev-task-reply (m input textrows cols rows)
  "A spawned task's reply (`:task-done` / `:task-error` / `:task-timeout`): route by which
in-flight handle it matches, through `ed-task-reply-table` (every feature's registered
rows). A handle matching none is a stale/cancelled reply and is dropped, so it can't
clobber a newer one.

A row registered `:folds-event?` (the C-x C-e eval, whose report depends on whether it
TIMED OUT or errored) gets the whole event; the rest get the value, or the row's failure
value when the task did not finish."
  (let (h (nth input 1)
        done? (= (first input) :task-done)
        table (ed-task-reply-table)
        slot (seq/find (keys table)
               (fn (k) (and (not (nil? (get m k))) (= (get m k) h)))))
    (if slot
      (let ([fold failed _label options] (get table slot))
        ((reflect/eval fold) m
          (cond
            (:folds-event? options) input
            done? (nth input 2)
            :else failed)))
      m)))

(defn- ed-ev-resize (m input textrows cols rows)
  "A `[:resize cols rows]`: re-clamp the scroll (vertical + horizontal) to the new
viewport, and tell any terminal buffer whose pane changed that its terminal changed size.

The second half matters because a pty child lays out against a size it is TOLD. It has
no other way to learn one — `iex` wraps its own input at the width it believes it has —
so a REPL opened in an 80-column window and then widened kept wrapping at 80 until it
was restarted.

`ed-terminals?` keeps a plain editing session from asking at all. The `unbound symbol:
term/term-sync-sizes` this guard was written against was the module's old NAME: `term`
resolved to std's `term` module, which has no such function (2026-10-18, renamed `terminal`)."
  (let (scrolled (ed-scroll-horizontal (ed-scroll m textrows) (ed-selected-text-cols m cols rows)))
    (if (ed-terminals? scrolled) (terminal/term-sync-sizes scrolled) scrolled)))

(defn- ed-mouse-on-click (m)
  "After a left-click moved point, run the current buffer mode's `:on-click` command (a
command symbol, resolved late like a keymap binding) — e.g. *git-status* visits the file
under the click. A no-op when the buffer's mode defines no `:on-click`."
  (let (svc (ed-mode-service (ed-current-buf m) :on-click))
    ;; a guest's session follows no click into another command (`model/ed-guest-allows?`)
    (if (or (nil? svc) (:guest-session m)) m (svc m :mouse))))

(defn- ed-drag-release (m input textrows cols rows row col)
  "End a left press over the text: a real drag-selection copies its region to the kill
ring + OS clipboard (Emacs `mouse-drag-copy-region`, in ANY buffer, keeping the region
highlighted); a plain CLICK (no region) runs the buffer mode's `:on-click` — so a
results-buffer row (`*Tests*` / `*git-status*` / `*Occur*` / `*compile*`) navigates to
its source ON CLICK, while a press-and-drag in the SAME buffer selects text instead.
That click-vs-drag split is what makes a results buffer's output highlightable/copyable
rather than every press being swallowed as a link jump.

A navigate then re-clamps the (now-selected) SOURCE pane so the visited line is actually
brought on screen — `ed-pop-to-file` only moves point and relies on this clamp, and unlike
the key path (RET) a mouse release returns straight out of here, so without it a click set
point but never scrolled the target into view. The copy path scrolls nothing (the region
stays put)."
  (let (folded (ed-mouse m input cols rows))
    ; ends the drag; the region (mark↔point) survives
    (if (nil? (buffer-region (ed-current-buf folded)))
      (let (navigated (ed-mouse-on-click folded))
        ; a plain click — navigate via :on-click (if any)
        (ed-scroll-horizontal (ed-scroll navigated textrows)
          (ed-selected-text-cols navigated cols rows)))
      (ed-drag-copy folded)))) ; a real region — copy it, leaving it selected
(defn- ed-menu-mouse-press (m cols rows row col)
  "A press while the context menu is open: hit-test via the view's widget zones
(`hittest/ed-menu-hit`) and drive the pure model op — run the enabled item, stay open on a
divider/disabled/padding click (`:inside`), dismiss on a click outside."
  (let (hit (ed-menu-hit m cols rows col row))
    ; hittest/ed-menu-hit — [:run i] | :inside | nil
    (cond
      (and (vector? hit) (= (first hit) :run)) (ed-menu-activate m (second hit))
      (= hit :inside) m
      else (ed-close-menu m))))

(defn- ed-menu-mouse-hover (m cols rows row col)
  "Pointer moved over the open menu: select the enabled item under it (standard dropdown
hover), else leave the selection put."
  (let (hit (ed-menu-hit m cols rows col row))
    (if (and (vector? hit) (= (first hit) :run)) (ed-menu-select m (second hit)) m)))

(defn- ed-ev-mouse (m input textrows cols rows)
  "A `[:mouse …]`: fold it via `ed-mouse`. A wheel notch scrolls/zooms the hovered pane,
and a scrollbar grab/drag/release moves the viewport directly — both leave point alone,
so they bypass the selected-pane point-clamp (`:sb-drag` spans the grab, so its press,
drags, and the release that clears it all skip the clamp); every other mouse action
re-clamps so a moved point stays on screen. A **right-press** opens the Copy/Cut/Paste
context menu at the pointer (`ed-open-menu`, point/region untouched); while a menu is open
it owns clicks — a press runs/hovers the item under it or dismisses it (`ed-menu-mouse-*`
over the view widget's zones)."
  (let (action (nth input 1) button (nth input 2) row (nth input 3) col (nth input 4))
    (cond
      ;; Bare hover (no button) folds into `:hover` for the status bar (the renderer draws the
      ;; hover pill + tooltip) — but must NOT re-clamp scroll: moving the mouse while you've
      ;; wheel-scrolled away from point would otherwise snap the view back to the cursor (the
      ;; GUI emits a :move per cell crossed).
      ;; An open context menu owns the mouse (modal): a press runs/dismisses, a move
      ;; hovers-to-select like a standard dropdown, everything else is swallowed. Checked
      ;; before the bare-move hover so the menu, not the status bar, gets the motion.
      (:menu m)
        (cond
          (= action :press) (ed-menu-mouse-press m cols rows row col)
          (= action :move) (ed-menu-mouse-hover m cols rows row col)
          else m)
      ;; the mode line is cells; the text under the pointer is found at its exact position
      ;; (`ed-mouse-at`), which a zoomed pane's rows need
      (= action :move)
        (let ([at-row at-col] (ed-mouse-at input))
          (ed-diag-hover (ed-link-hover (ed-modeline-hover m cols rows col row)
                           cols rows at-col at-row)
            cols rows at-col at-row))
      (and (= action :press) (= button :right)) (ed-open-menu m row col)
      ;; Releasing a left press: a real drag-selection copies its region; a plain click
      ;; runs the row's `:on-click` (a results buffer navigates to its source ON CLICK).
      ;; Navigation is here on RELEASE, not on press, so a press begins a drag-select
      ;; instead of being swallowed as a link jump — that's what lets you highlight and
      ;; copy text in *Tests* / *git-status* / *Occur* / *compile*.
      (and (= action :release) (:text-drag m)) (ed-drag-release m input textrows cols rows row col)
      ;; A left-press on a clickable status-bar segment (the branch / git indicator) runs its
      ;; command instead of the normal press handling — so it opens *git-status*, not moves
      ;; point. `ed-modeline-click` is nil unless a segment with an `:on-click` is under it.
      :else
        (let (mclick (if (and (= action :press) (= button :left))
                       (ed-modeline-click m cols rows col row)
                       nil))
          (if mclick
            mclick
            (let (folded (ed-mouse m input cols rows))
              ;; NB: a results row's `:on-click` navigation fires on the click's RELEASE
              ;; (ed-drag-release), NOT here on press — so the press can begin a
              ;; drag-select rather than steal it as a link click.
              (if (or (= action :scroll-up) (= action :scroll-down) (:sb-drag m) (:sb-drag folded))
                folded
                (ed-scroll-horizontal (ed-scroll folded textrows)
                  (ed-selected-text-cols folded cols rows)))))))))

(defn- ed-ev-buffer-updated (m input textrows cols rows)
  "A shared buffer process's push (`[:buffer-updated proc version text]`, from
`subscribe-buffer` — see `src/collab.blsp`): fold the new content into the pool slot
linked to `proc`, leaving every pane's window-local point alone (cursors stay
independent). Stale versions and pushes for an unlinked process are ignored inside
`ed-apply-buffer-update`. While FOLLOWING a participant, point then rides their caret
(`collab-follow-jump` — the one point-move here, scroll-clamped). Otherwise no scroll
clamp: content changed under the view, point didn't move — the render clamps points
into the new text."
  (collab-follow-jump (ed-apply-buffer-update m
                        (nth input 1)
                        (nth input 2)
                        (nth input 3)
                        (nth input 4 nil))
    textrows))

(defn- ed-ev-down (m input textrows cols rows)
  "A monitored process died (`[:down ref pid reason]` — `ed-host-slot` monitors every
hosted buffer process). If `pid` backs a pool slot, recover it: a SHARED slot re-asks
the registry (which respawns died buffers from its text mirror) or falls back to a
local host — `collab-reshare`; a local slot respawns from the pool cache —
`hosted-rehost`. The per-buffer fault boundary: one crashed buffer process costs at
most its in-flight splices, never the editor. A pid backing no slot is offered to the
features that keep processes of their own (`model/register-down-handler`: a language
server's connection)."
  (let (pid (nth input 2)
        i (hosted-slot-for-proc m pid))
    (cond
      ;; one of the primary's frames closed: forget it (src/frames.blsp)
      (includes? (or (:frames m) []) pid) ((reflect/eval 'frames/frames-forget) m pid)
      (nil? i) (ed-run-down-handlers m pid (nth input 3 nil))
      ;; a registry-hosted slot re-shares through the registry (`hosted-share-slot`): the
      ;; first frame to notice respawns from its cache, the others land on that process
      (get-in m [:hosted i :registry?]) (hosted-rehost m i)
      (get-in m [:hosted i :collab?]) (collab-reshare m i)
      :else (hosted-rehost m i))))

(defn- ed-ev-client-opts (m input textrows cols rows)
  "The attach identity (`[:client-opts {:name …}]`, queued by a serve session before any
key — std/editor/serve): record the participant name and announce it on every shared
buffer process, so collab presence (name tags, join/leave echoes) shows who this is.
A nil/empty name is ignored. A guest (`:guest` — brood's `editor/serve` marks a client that
attached over a guest link) gets a guest's session: the allowlist `model/ed-guest-allows?`."
  (let (opts (nth input 1)
        named (collab-set-name m (:name opts)))
    (if (= true (:guest opts)) (assoc named :guest-session true) named)))

(defn- ed-ev-key-up (m input textrows cols rows)
  "A `[:key-up k]` (a window, or a terminal once a held key stops): nothing to do — the
frontend repeats a held key, so its release stops nothing here. Handled so the vector is
not dispatched as a key."
  m)

(defn- ed-ev-ranked (m input textrows cols rows)
  "A ranking session's answer (`[:fuzzy-ranked <session> query hits]`, std/fuzzy's `ranker`):
fold it into the open minibuffer's candidate cache. Dropped unless it came from THIS prompt's
own session — a reply arriving after its prompt closed must not repopulate the next one."
  (ed-apply-ranked m (nth input 1) (nth input 2) (nth input 3)))

(def- ed-event-handlers
  ;; `[:tag …]` -> the handler's SYMBOL, resolved at dispatch (`(reflect/eval …)`, below) — the
  ;; same late binding keys get: redefine a handler in the running editor and the next
  ;; event of that tag uses it, with no reload. Function *values* here would freeze the
  ;; async half of the loop at module-load time while the key half stayed hot-swappable
  ;; (and would hide every handler from `nest test --cover`, whose instrumentation
  ;; rebinds the global that a captured value has already bypassed).
  ;;
  ;; These are the events the loop itself consumes. A feature's own — a language server's
  ;; replies, another frame's buffer registry — are registered by the feature when it loads
  ;; (`model/register-event-handler`) and looked up beside these ([[ed-event-handler]]).
  {:io/write 'input/ed-ev-io-write
   :key-up 'input/ed-ev-key-up
   :fuzzy-ranked 'input/ed-ev-ranked
   :task-done 'input/ed-ev-task-reply
   :task-error 'input/ed-ev-task-reply
   :task-timeout 'input/ed-ev-task-reply
   :resize 'input/ed-ev-resize
   :mouse 'input/ed-ev-mouse
   :buffer-updated 'input/ed-ev-buffer-updated
   :down 'input/ed-ev-down
   :client-opts 'input/ed-ev-client-opts})

(defn ed-event-handler (tag)
  "The handler symbol for inbound `[tag …]` events — the loop's own ([[ed-event-handlers]])
or one a feature registered (`model/register-event-handler`) — or nil when none is."
  (or (get ed-event-handlers tag) (ed-registered-event-handler tag)))

(defn ed-system-event? (input)
  "Whether `input` is an inbound `[:tag …]` event with a registered handler."
  (and (vector? input) (not (nil? (ed-event-handler (first input))))))

;; ---- the ui-run update ------------------------------------------------------

(defn- ed-macro-record (m input)
  "While a keyboard macro is recording (`:macro-recording` is a vector), append the raw key
`input` to it — skipping timers, focus events, inbound `[:tag …]` system events AND
`UiEvent` records (only real keystrokes belong in a macro; a replayed async event
would re-append phantom report lines and resurrect dead pids). Text an input method
committed (`[:text s]`) is typing, so it is kept; a composition still in progress
(`[:preedit …]`) is not, since only its commit changes the buffer. The C-x ) that ends
recording is captured too and trimmed by `cmd-end-kbd-macro`."
  (if (and (vector? (:macro-recording m))
        (not (= input :blur))
        (not (= input :focus-in))
        (not (= input :close))
        (or (not (vector? input)) (= (first input) :text))
        (not (record? input)))
    ;; `conj` appends to the vector in place of the old one-element `into`, which built a
    ;; list and walked it per key
    (assoc m :macro-recording (conj (:macro-recording m) input))
    m))

(def *view-resume-keys*
  ;; Plain cursor-movement keys that should resume from the VISIBLE area after you've
  ;; scrolled the cursor off-screen (wheel / scrollbar): they pull point into view first
  ;; (`ed-point-into-view`), so the view doesn't snap back to the off-screen cursor — what
  ;; reads as "jumping to the top". Everything else (recenter C-l, goto, isearch, page
  ;; scroll, typing, …) deliberately acts on the real point, off-screen or not.
  [:up :down :left :right :ctrl-n :ctrl-p :ctrl-f :ctrl-b])

(sig ed-point-into-view (model int -> model))
(defn- ed-point-into-view (m textrows)
  "If the selected pane's point has scrolled off-screen — the user wheeled or dragged the
view away from the cursor — move point to the nearest visible line (keeping its column), so
the next keystroke continues from what's on screen rather than snapping the whole view back
to the off-screen cursor (which, with point near the top, reads as 'jumping to the top').
A no-op when point is already visible, so normal editing is untouched."
  (let (buf (ed-current-buf m)
        top (ed-current-top m)
        cl (buffer-current-line buf)
        ;; the last line on screen: `top + rows - 1`, or for a row-wise buffer the last
        ;; line whose TEXT is in its row table (a long line above takes several rows, an
        ;; inlay takes one, and a trailing inlay row shows a line's note, not the line)
        w (or (:view-cols m) 0)
        inlays (ed-row-extras buf)
        bot (if (and (ed-row-wise? buf inlays) (> w 0))
              (let (text-rows (seq/filter (wrap/rows-from buf top w textrows inlays)
                                (fn (row) (nil? (nth row 3)))))
                (if (empty? text-rows) top (first (last text-rows))))
              (+ top (dec textrows))))
    (if (and (>= cl top) (<= cl bot))
      m
      (ed-set-buf m (forward-line buf (- (math/max top (math/min bot cl)) cl))))))

(defn- ed-refresh-spans-guarded (m cols rows)
  "The loop-tail span refresh (`panes/ed-refresh-spans`), which may NOT veto the event it
follows. A lexer or restart that throws — a mode naming a service its module never defined
(`unbound symbol: editor/lexer/line-restart`, 2026-09-15) — is reported in the echo area and
the command's model stands, `:done` included. Unguarded, the refresh's throw reached the
dispatch guard below, which keeps the PRE-event model: every input was silently dropped,
the close request among them, and the window could not be shut. `ed-view` guards the same
failure on the paint side (the `render error` frame)."
  (try
    (ed-refresh-spans m cols rows)
    (catch e (message m (str "fontify error: " (error-message e))))))

(defn- ed-fold-input (m0 input textrows textcols cols rows)
  "Fold one input into the model, by what kind of input it is: a named timer, a bare tick, the
window's close/blur, a `UiEvent` record, an inbound `[:tag …]` system event, or — anything
else — a key."
  (cond
    ;; a named timer fired (ADR-102): debounce / which-key / undo / spinner / glide. Must
    ;; precede `ed-system-event?` — `[:timer …]` is a vector but isn't in the event-handler
    ;; registry, so it'd otherwise fall through to key dispatch.
    (ed-timer-event? input) (ed-timer-fire m0 (nth input 1) textrows textcols cols rows)
    ;; the window's X button (`:close`, claimed via `:close-is-input?`): the same command as
    ;; C-x C-c, so unsaved work is asked about first. Late-bound like the event handlers, and
    ;; outside any transient — a close is never a key.
    (= input :close) ((reflect/eval 'commands/cmd-quit) m0 input)
    ;; the window lost focus (`:blur`): the caret goes hollow, and a composition left showing
    ;; goes with it — not every input method says it ended
    (= input :blur) (assoc (dissoc m0 :preedit) :unfocused true)
    ;; and back: the caret is solid again (brood ADR-441's `:focus-in`), and the window
    ;; blinks it again (it held it shown while unfocused, brood ADR-469)
    (= input :focus-in) (dissoc m0 :unfocused)
    ;; Text that is more than one key's worth (an input method's commit, a compose sequence,
    ;; brood ADR-441): each character takes the key path, as though typed, so the minibuffer,
    ;; isearch and a terminal's program all get it the way they get typing, and the composition
    ;; shown at point ends.
    (and (vector? input) (= (first input) :text))
      (fold (string/->graphemes (nth input 1))
        (dissoc m0 :preedit)
        (fn (acc character) (ed-key-step acc character textrows textcols)))
    ;; The input method's composition in progress, shown at point and not yet inserted
    ;; (`[:preedit text range]`); an empty text clears it.
    (and (vector? input) (= (first input) :preedit))
      (if (= (nth input 1) "")
        (dissoc m0 :preedit)
        (assoc m0 :preedit {:text (nth input 1) :range (nth input 2 nil)}))
    ;; an app-produced async event record → it folds itself in (`UiEvent`, see model.blsp —
    ;; no handler table to consult).
    (ui-event? input) (ui-event-fold input m0 textrows cols rows)
    ;; an inbound async/system event (`[:tag …]`) → its registered handler. A MOUSE gesture
    ;; can edit the buffer (context-menu Paste/Cut, a drag-kill), so the buffer's `:post-key`
    ;; guards apply to it exactly as to a key — without this, a right-click Paste bypassed the
    ;; tutorial's prose protection. Other system events (task replies, subprocess lines) never
    ;; edit interactively and skip the guards.
    (ed-system-event? input)
      (let (folded ((reflect/eval (ed-event-handler (first input))) m0
                     input textrows cols rows))
        (if (= (first input) :mouse) (ed-post-key-guards m0 folded input) folded))
    ;; otherwise a key: for a plain cursor-movement key, first pull point back into view if
    ;; the user scrolled it off-screen (so the move resumes from what's visible instead of
    ;; snapping the view to the off-screen cursor) — skipped while a transient overlay owns
    ;; input, and NOT for other commands (recenter/goto/search act on the real point). Then
    ;; route through the keymap (`ed-key-step`, which also runs the mode `:post-key`
    ;; guards). A key that reaches the editor is not part of a
    ;; composition, so one still showing (a cancelled one, C-g) is cleared.
    else
      (ed-key-step (let (typed (if (:preedit m0) (dissoc m0 :preedit) m0))
                     (if (and (includes? *view-resume-keys* input)
                           (nil? (overlay-active ed-transient-handlers typed)))
                       (ed-point-into-view typed textrows)
                       typed))
        input textrows textcols)))

(defn- ed-loop-tail (m0 result input cols rows)
  "After the event folded in: replay a keyboard macro if one was asked for, else propagate.

C-x e (cmd-execute-kbd-macro) set `:run-macro`: replay the recorded macro by re-feeding its
events through `ed-update` (recording is off, so they aren't re-captured). The replayed calls
each run their OWN collab chain, so the replay path must NOT be wrapped in another
`collab-step` — the outer propagate would diff the pre-replay model against the final one and
send the macro's whole change a SECOND time.

Otherwise the hosted content propagate (`hosted-step` — ships this event's edits to every
process-backed slot, shared or purely local), then the collab presence chain (`collab-step` —
auto-share / follow / presence sync). Each is a map-lookup no-op on a model with nothing
hosted, which is what lets `share-session` turn a LIVE editor into a host without swapping
update fns."
  (if (:run-macro result)
    (fold (or (:macro-last result) '())
      (dissoc result :run-macro)
      (fn (mm ev) (ed-update mm ev cols rows)))
    (collab-step m0 (hosted-step m0 result input) input)))

(defn- ed-recover (m0 e input)
  "A thrown error — from a command, the minibuffer, completion, or a re-check — surfaces in the
echo area and the editor keeps running: a crash must never take down the whole editor (ui-run
would otherwise restore the screen and re-raise, exiting). The pre-event model `m0` stands
(the edit is undone by being abandoned — the buffer is immutable).

A read-only span refusing an edit is not an error to surface as one: `ed-edit` already turns
the common path into the buffer's hint, and this is the SAFETY NET for any command that edits
some other way — a `:kind :read-only` throw (std/editor/buffer) degrades to the same hint,
never a raw `error:`.

The error echo appends to *Messages*, which is HOSTED under the flip — the append must still
propagate or the pool cache silently diverges from the *Messages* process (its own guarded
try: a propagate error here must not recurse into another echo)."
  (let (me (if (and (map? e) (= (:kind e) :read-only))
             (message m0
               (or (ed-mode-service (ed-current-buf m0) :read-only-hint) "read-only text here"))
             (message m0 (str "error: " (error-message e)))))
    (try (hosted-step m0 me input) (catch e2 me))))

(defn- ed-sync-title (result)
  ;; Update the OS title bar from the result model every event (cheap: a string set).
  (let (win (:window result)
        title (ed-window-title result))
    (when (and win (not (= title (:window-title result))))
      (theme/gui-only gui/title win title))
    (assoc result :window-title title)))

(defn- ed-key-up? (input)
  "True if `input` is a key's release (`[:key-up k]`)."
  (and (vector? input) (= (first input) :key-up)))

(defn- ed-update-turn (m input cols rows)
  "The whole update: fold one key into the next model, then keep point on screen.
A `[:resize cols rows]` event just re-clamps the scroll to the new viewport and lets
the loop re-render (it isn't a command, so it doesn't touch the keymap or echo); a
`[:mouse …]` event is handled by `ed-mouse` (left-click selects the window under it,
a press on a divider begins a drag, `:drag` resizes it, `:release` ends it). Scroll
clamping uses the *selected pane's* height (`ed-selected-text-rows`), so it's correct
inside a split. Any non-system input is a key, routed by `ed-route-transient`: to the
open transient overlay (minibuffer / completion / isearch / query-replace) if one
owns it, else through the keymap (`ed-dispatch`).

The pipeline: [[ed-fold-input]], then `ed-post-step` schedules the debounced eldoc +
diagnostics check (off the per-key path — see model.blsp) and `ed-refresh-spans` refreshes
the fontify span cache for the new viewport (a no-op when nothing scrolled or changed — see
panes.blsp), then [[ed-loop-tail]]; a throw anywhere is [[ed-recover]]ed. The diff-hl git
annotation and the web-mirror snapshot push are NOT done on this per-event path — both can be
slow (a full-file diff; a fontify), and doing them per keystroke once wedged the whole loop on
a large file. They run debounced on the idle beat instead (`ed-timer-fire :idle-recompute`)."
  (let ([textrows textcols] (ed-viewport m cols rows)
        ;; …and the WHOLE frame beside them (`:frame`), which is a different question: the
        ;; view rows are the selected pane's, and laying panes out (`ed-body-layout`) needs
        ;; the window. Anything reached OFF the render path — an async reply that appends to
        ;; a streaming buffer and wants to follow its tail — has no other way to ask, and
        ;; every such consumer inventing its own stash is how a model grows three of them.
        ;; The viewport goes on the model so page commands know it; the key is captured if a
        ;; macro is recording.
        m0 (ed-macro-record (assoc m :view-rows textrows :view-cols textcols :frame [cols rows])
             input))
    ;; a committed turn frees the tree handles it dropped (`syntaxtree`); a rolled-back one
    ;; still names them
    (ed-sync-title (syntaxtree/ed-release-dropped-trees m
                     (try
                       (ed-loop-tail m0
                         (ed-refresh-spans-guarded (ed-post-step (ed-fold-input m0
                                                                   input textrows textcols cols rows)
                                                     input)
                           cols rows)
                         input
                         cols
                         rows)
                       (catch e (ed-recover m0 e input)))))))

(defn ed-update (m input cols rows)
  "ui-run update: fold one input into the next model — the whole pipeline
([[ed-update-turn]]) unless the input can change nothing it would act on. Three can't:

- a key's release (`[:key-up k]`): bedit keeps no state about a held key (the desktop
  repeats it, brood ADR-441, and `ui-run` holds the idle timers while one is down), so `m`
  comes back as it was;
- our OWN edit echoed back by a hosted buffer's process (`hosted/hosted-own-echo?`): the
  pool already has it, so `m` comes back as it was;
- a [[*cosmetic-timers*]] beat (the spinner, the scrollbar fade): only the
  field it animates changes ([[ed-cosmetic-fire]]).

Handing `m` back untouched is what lets `ui-run`'s `=` test see a turn that changed nothing
and draw nothing; before this our own echo cost every keystroke on a hosted buffer a second
full update and a second frame.

Only a model that has had one full turn takes the short path (`:timers-synced?`, set by
`model/ed-sync-timers`): the model `main` starts the loop with was built outside it, and its
idle beat — the git summary, diff-hl, the diagnostics — is armed by the first full turn. When
the first input was a cosmetic tick, that turn never came, and an editor left alone at launch never
showed its branch."
  (cond
    (not (:timers-synced? m)) (ed-update-turn m input cols rows)
    (ed-key-up? input) m
    (hosted-own-echo? m input) m
    (ed-cosmetic-timer? input) (ed-cosmetic-fire m (nth input 1))
    else (ed-update-turn m input cols rows)))

;; The loop's update contract, in types. `assoc` answers `vector | map` — a receiver's
;; type does not survive an update — so the closing `(assoc result :window-title title)`
;; hands back the WIDTH rather than the model, and every caller that threads two events
;; through `ed-update` re-reports it — mostly the tests, which thread several. Declaring it
;; took `nest check --strict` from 461 findings to 368. std/editor/buffer declares the same
;; thing for every buffer transformer (`(sig set-mark (buffer -> buffer))`) for this reason.
;;
;; Declaring it moves the report to this line (`--strict` says the body yields the width)
;; and that is the whole point: ONE place says "the checker cannot see through `assoc`
;; here", instead of every caller saying it. Closing it for real means declaring the
;; `model -> model` contract through the interior too (`ed-timer-fire`, `ui-event-fold`,
;; `collab-step`, `hosted-step`, `message`, …) — a chain that has to be typed whole, since
;; a narrow sig on an interior node with untyped callers just moves the width reports
;; upstream (measured: four interior sigs took --strict from 368 back up to 438).
(sig ed-update (model any int int -> model))
